diff --git a/doc/spec.md b/doc/spec.md
index 29a38cb..175ec88 100644
--- a/doc/spec.md
+++ b/doc/spec.md
@@ -2870,6 +2870,34 @@ load("module.star", "x", y2="y", "z")    # assigns x, y2, and z
 
 A load statement may not be nested inside any other statement.
 
+<b>Implementation note:</b>
+The Go implementation of Starlark permits an additional form of the
+load statement when the `LoadModule` file option is enabled.
+In this form, the load appears on the right-hand side of an assignment
+to a single identifier:
+
+```grammar {.good}
+LoadModuleStmt = identifier '=' 'load' '(' string {',' [identifier '='] string} [','] ')' .
+```
+
+Whereas an ordinary load statement binds the listed names in the file
+scope, this form binds them as attributes of a single value of type
+`module`, which it assigns to the identifier.
+If no names are listed, the module value has an attribute for each of
+the loaded module's exported globals, that is, those whose names do not
+start with `_`:
+
+```python
+lib = load("lib.star")                 # lib.f, lib.g, ... (all exported names)
+lib = load("lib.star", "f", h="g")     # lib.f and lib.h only
+lib.f(1)
+lib._g                                 # error: module has no ._g field or method
+```
+
+Like the names bound by an ordinary load statement, the identifier is
+local to the file, and the statement may not be nested inside any
+other statement. `load` is not otherwise an expression:
+`f(load("lib.star"))` is a syntax error.
 
 ## Module execution
 
